--- title: "Warning: Six New Danfoss Vulnerabilities - Refrigeration Controllers at Risk - Immediate Action Required!" url: https://ccb.belgium.be/advisories/warning-six-new-danfoss-vulnerabilities-refrigeration-controllers-risk-immediate-action hostname: belgium.be sitename: ccb.belgium.be date: "2026-11-12" fingerprint: b7507283c06975cc --- # Warning: Six New Danfoss Vulnerabilities - Refrigeration Controllers at Risk - Immediate Action Required! **Last update:** 12/11/2026 **Affected product:** Danfoss - AK-SM800A **Affected versions:** All up to and including 5.3.2 **Type:** Stack-based buffer overflows **Potential impact:** Full device take-over, Persistent access (possibly after reset), Persistent Denial of Service (rendering device inoperable) ## Source **Midnight Blue** - [https://www.midnightblue.nl/blog/multiple-rce-danfoss-aksm800a](https://www.midnightblue.nl/blog/multiple-rce-danfoss-aksm800a) ## Risk The Centre for Cybersecurity Belgium (CCB) has been informed of multiple incidents involving refrigeration systems in Belgium. Following an initial wave of attacks targeting remote refrigeration controllers in France beginning on 11 October 2026, a second wave targeted supermarkets and cold-storage operations across Europe from 2 November 2026. These incidents involve internet-exposed management interfaces, including Danfoss, Dixell xweb, and Copeland xweb devices, and have resulted in unwanted defrosting operations, stock destruction, and supply disruptions. More information is available in our previous flash alerts. New research published by Midnight Blue reports **six zero-day vulnerabilities allowing pre-authentication remote code execution (RCE) in Danfoss AK-SM800A controllers**, affecting firmware versions up to and including 5.3.2. **An attacker could exploit these vulnerabilities without logging in or possessing valid credentials**, potentially gaining full control of the affected device. CVE identifiers were pending at the time of publication, and coordinated disclosure with Danfoss was ongoing. No patches were available at that time. **Removing direct public internet exposure is the immediate priority and must not be delayed while awaiting patches.** Updating firmware, changing default passwords, and enabling strict session control are insufficient on their own to secure internet-exposed controllers. The research also identifies a vulnerability in the session control mechanism itself, meaning that strict mode does not eliminate the risk. The researchers further warn that attackers could potentially establish persistence that survives a factory reset or firmware update, or render controllers inoperable. ## Call to Action All organisations using refrigeration controllers should immediately identify and remove direct internet exposure of their management interfaces. This action remains necessary even where firmware has been updated, default passwords have been changed, or strict session control has been enabled. A 4G or 5G modem alone is not a security measure. If the controller’s management interface remains accessible from the public internet through that connection, it remains exposed. Remote access should be restricted through a secure, up-to-date VPN gateway or a protected private network. Organisations are requested to follow the recommendations below and report any confirmed compromises to the CCB. ## Recommended Actions The Centre for Cybersecurity Belgium recommends: - **Remove Internet Exposure (only real solution):** Ensure management interfaces cannot be reached directly from the public internet. Restrict remote access to a secure VPN or a protected internal network. - **Update and Secure:** Monitor for security patches and upgrade as soon as they become available. At the time of writing, no patches were available for these newly disclosed vulnerabilities. Removing direct public internet exposure is the immediate priority and must not be delayed while awaiting patches. - **Verify Configuration:** Check the entire configuration, particularly temperature settings, defrost schedules, user accounts, and remote-access settings. - **Contractual Review:** Review contractual and insurance requirements before interrupting data reporting to the refrigeration service provider. Arrange protected remote access so that essential monitoring can continue. - **Temporary Mitigation:** In an emergency, simple filtering of the source IPs of refrigeration providers on a firewall is possible, but this should not be considered a sustainable solution. **Note:** Internet-exposed refrigeration controllers remain at risk, including devices running the latest firmware assessed in the research. End-of-Life equipment should be prioritised for replacement. Removing direct internet exposure must not be delayed while awaiting patches. In case of a confirmed intrusion, [please report the incident](https://ccb.belgium.be/en/cert/report-incident) via the CCB portal.
